    "notes": "Beatrice became the key stabilizing figure in Luxembourg after Henry VI's death at Worringen. Her regency protected the position of the minor Henry VII, who later became Roman-German king and Holy Roman Emperor.\n\nConfidence / caveats: Her birth year is uncertain and is variously reconstructed within the 1250s. The exact marriage date is also uncertain. The end of her guardianship was gradual rather than a sharply defined constitutional date; Henry VII was governing independently by the mid-1290s. Older sources give different totals for her daughters, so only the consistently named children are listed.\n\nUnlinked family: Baldwin of Avesnes, Lord of Beaumont (not uniquely matched in the roster); Felicitas of Coucy (not uniquely matched in the roster); Baldwin of Luxembourg (not uniquely matched in the roster); Felicitas of Luxembourg (not uniquely matched in the roster); Margaret of Luxembourg (not uniquely matched in the roster).",
